'''Dr. Jermaine Lafrance Johnson Sr.''' (born July 4, 1985) is an American [[politician]], [[political advisor]] and former professional [[basketball]] player. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he is the member for [[South Carolina House of Representatives]] representing the 52nd district.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Election Night Reporting |url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/122436/web.345435/#/detail/28104 |access-date=November 8, 2024 |website=www.enr-scvotes.org |language=en}}</ref>

Johnson earned degrees from the [[College of Charleston]], [[Strayer University]], and [[Northcentral University]]. Before entering politics he played professional basketball internationally and founded the New Economic Beginnings Foundation, an organization focused on providing educational and employment opportunities within the [[South Carolina Democratic Party]], and served as South Carolina campaign chair for [[Andrew Yang]]'s 2020 presidential campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/ |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref> As a state representative, Johnson has participated in legislative work involving education, public works, labor, commerce, and industry, and has supported efforts to recognize the contributions of historical figures such as [[Robert Smalls]].[https://www.admin.sc.gov/robertsmalls] 

==Early life and education==
Johnson was born on July 4, 1985, in [[Los Angeles, California]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Carolina Legislature Online - Member Biography |url=https://www.scstatehouse.gov/member.php?code=943181705 |access-date=2026-01-29 |website=www.scstatehouse.gov}}</ref> During his childhood he experienced significant economic hardship, including periods of housing instability that led his family to live in motels following evictions. His father spent time in prison, and his brother served in the military.<ref name="Post Courier">{{Cite web|last=Caraviello|first=David|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/from-homeless-youth-and-turbulent-cofc-career-jermaine-johnson-is/article_cfb4f2a2-01ad-11ea-bdd4-ff23e4b5af2c.html|title=From homeless youth and turbulent CofC career, Jermaine Johnson is Andrew Yang's guy in SC|work=[[The Post and Courier]]|access-date=March 30, 2020|date=November 29, 2019}}</ref>

Johnson attended multiple high schools, including [[Trinity-Pawling School]], and Redemption Christian Academy, before graduating from the [[The Winchendon School|The Winchendon School.]] His involvement in basketball helped him gain access to preparatory schools, and pursue opportunities in higher education.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Courier |first=David Caraviello Special to The Post and |date=2019-11-29 |title=From homeless youth and turbulent CofC career, Jermaine Johnson is Andrew Yang's guy in SC |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/from-homeless-youth-and-turbulent-cofc-career-jermaine-johnson-is-andrew-yangs-guy-in-sc/article_cfb4f2a2-01ad-11ea-bdd4-ff23e4b5af2c.html |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref> 

Johnson earned a Bachelor of Arts from the College of Charleston in 2008, a Master of Science in Management from Strayer University in 2014, and a Doctorate of Business Administration and Organizational Leadership from [[Northcentral University]] in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robertson |first=Mike |date=2021-06-28 |title=CofC Alumnus Jermaine Johnson is a Game Changer |url=https://today.cofc.edu/2021/06/28/jermaine-johnson-south-carolina-state-representative/ |access-date=2026-01-29 |website=The College Today |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kesler |first=Alex |date=2025-10-28 |title=Jermaine Johnson announces bid for S.C. governor |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/state/jermaine-johnson-announces-bid-for-sc-governor-alan-wilson-pamela-evette-nancy-mace-josh-kimbrell-ralph-norman |access-date=2026-01-29 |website=WCIV |language=en}}</ref>

==Athletic career==
===Amateur career===
Johnson developed his basketball skills through the [[Amateur Athletic Union]], where his coach helped him pursue opportunities to attend preparatory schools. 
 
In high school, Johnson committed to the [[College of Charleston]]. Three months into his redshirt freshman season, Johnson got into an altercation with his teammate Jeff Horowitz. Johnson intended to transfer to Long Beach State, but ended up staying at CofC.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://uncwsports.com/news/2006/11/26/Draper_Cougars_Speed_Past_Seahawks_91_70.aspx|title=Draper, Cougars Speed Past Seahawks, 91–70|website=University of North Carolina Wilmington|access-date=March 30, 2020}}</ref>

In 2008, Johnson scored the first points in the [[TD Arena]] for the Cougars.

Johnson served as Charleston's captain during his senior year. He finished his career as one of only four players in school history with at least 1,100 points and 850 rebounds.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nmnathletics.com/ViewArticle.dbml?DB_OEM_ID=14800&ATCLID=781024|title=CofC Men's Basketball BIO|website=College of Charleston|access-date=March 30, 2020}}{{Dead link|date=October 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

===Professional career===
The [[Reno Bighorns]] selected Johnson in the sixth round of the NBA Development Draft. He was released from the team after one year. He trained with [[James Harden]] and [[Amar'e Stoudemire]] during summer workouts in Los Angeles.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://cofcsports.com/news/2009/11/6/204828434.aspx|title=Former CofC Standout Jermaine Johnson Selected in NBA D-League Draft|website=College of Charleston|access-date=March 30, 2020}}</ref>

Johnson played more than six years professionally overseas, including in France, Mexico, Hungary and Canada. He starred during his time in the [[Mexican Basketball League]] where he averaged 21 points and 8 rebounds per game.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.interperformances.com/news/skyhawks-sign-jermaine-johnson-2013-09-17/|title=SKYHAWKS SIGN JERMAINE JOHNSON
|website=Interperformances|access-date=March 30, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/mens-college-basketball/player/_/id/22893/jermaine-johnson|title=JERMAINE JOHNSON|website=[[ESPN]]|access-date=March 30, 2020}}</ref>

==Political career==

===South Carolina House of Representatives===

In 2020, Johnson announced he would challenge Democratic incumbent [[Jimmy Bales]] for his seat in the South Carolina House of Representatives. Johnson centered his campaign around [[universal basic income]], community investment, economic investment, district enhancement and access and accountability. He earned endorsements from former 2020 presidential candidate [[Andrew Yang]], former South Carolina State Representative and current CNN political analyst [[Bakari Sellers]] and current South Carolina State Representative [[JA Moore (politician)|JA Moore]]. In June 2020, Johnson defeated Bales in the Democratic Primary and became the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/free-times/news/local_and_state_news/democrat-jermaine-johnson-to-challenge-for-state-house-seat-currently/article_ec57ac3e-6a0c-11ea-954f-a74ef6603941.html|title=Democrat Jermaine Johnson to challenge for state House seat currently held by Jimmy Bales|website=[[The Post and Courier]]|access-date=March 30, 2020}}</ref>

After [[redistricting]] following the [[2020 United States census]], Johnson's district was merged into House District 70. This forced Johnson to run in a contested primary against fellow-representative [[Wendy Brawley]]. In the primary, Johnson garnered 50.11% percent of the vote to defeat Brawley by 115 votes.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article262344482.html|title=In showdown between 2 sitting SC House members, Johnson narrowly wins District 70 race|website=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]]|access-date=May 16, 2022}}</ref>

Johnson currently serves on the House Labor, Commerce and Industry Committee. Past service includes the House Education and Public Works Committee.<ref>"House Standing Committees". ''South Carolina Legislature''. December 7, 2022. Retrieved December 8, 2022.</ref> He is Secretary of the House Minority Caucus.<ref>"Our Leadership". ''South Carolina House Democratic Caucus''. 2022. Retrieved December 13, 2022.</ref>

In 2023, Johnson co-sponsored legislation with Representative [[Brandon Cox (politician)|Brandon Cox]] to place a monument on the South Carolina State House grounds honoring [[Robert Smalls]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clervaud |first=Fraendy |date=May 27, 2024 |title=Monument for formerly enslaved man turned war hero, coming to SC State House |url=https://wach.com/news/local/monument-for-formerly-enslaved-man-turned-war-hero-coming-to-sc-state-house-robert-smalls-civil-war-beaufort-jermaine-johnson-brandon-cox |access-date=August 30, 2024 |website=WACH |language=en}}</ref> In 2024, Johnson was among the state legislators appointed to serve on the [[Robert Smalls]] Monument Commission.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Frazier |first=Herb |date=August 23, 2024 |title=S.C. forms Smalls Monument Commission |url=https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2024/08/23/s-c-forms-smalls-monument-commission/ |access-date=August 30, 2024 |website=Charleston City Paper |language=en-US}}</ref>

On January 14, 2026, Johnson announced that he would not run for re-election to the South Carolina House of Representatives, opting to focus instead on his gubernatorial run.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kayanja |first=Ian |date=2026-01-14 |title=State Rep. Johnson not seeking re-election in SC House District 52 |url=https://wpde.com/news/local/state-rep-johnson-not-seeking-re-election-in-sc-house-district-52 |access-date=2026-01-15 |website=WPDE |language=en}}</ref>

=== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ===
{{Main|2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election}}

On May 31, 2025, Johnson formed an [[exploratory committee]] to consider a run in the [[2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bustos |first1=Joseph |title=This Democratic state representative is now formally exploring a run for governor |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article307558936.html |access-date=May 31, 2025 |work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |date=May 31, 2025}}</ref> On October 28, 2025, he announced his candidacy.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Brams |first1=Sophie |title=Democratic State Rep. Jermaine Johnson enters race to become South Carolina’s next governor |url=https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/democratic-state-rep-jermaine-johnson-enters-race-to-become-south-carolinas-next-governor/ |access-date=October 28, 2025 |work=[[WCBD]] |date=October 28, 2025}}</ref>

In March 2026, rumors surfaced that Johnson was planning to quit the gubernatorial race, due to viability concerns related to fundraising, race and the possible entry of former [[Bill Clinton]] and [[Dick Riley]] administration official Billy Webster into the Democratic primary.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-03-04 |title=Upstate Businessman to Seek South Carolina Governor's Office - FITSNews |url=https://www.fitsnews.com/2026/03/04/upstate-businessman-to-seek-south-carolina-governors-office/ |access-date=2026-03-22 |language=en-US}}</ref> On March 21, and in a March 22 interview,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hemphill |first=Joshua |date=2026-03-23 |title=Rep. Jermaine Johnson speaks on plans to stay in gubernatorial race, to officially file Monday |url=https://www.wistv.com/2026/03/23/rep-jermaine-johnson-speaks-plans-stay-gubernatorial-race-officially-file-monday/ |access-date=2026-03-23 |website=WIS-TV |language=en}}</ref> Johnson confirmed that he had considered quitting but was encouraged by supporters to remain in the race and [[South Carolina Democratic Party]] Chair [[Christale Spain]] disputed claims that the state party was behind efforts to push Johnson out.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kayanja |first=Ian |date=2026-03-21 |title=State Rep. Johnson says he’s staying in governor’s race amid rumors of dropping bid |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/state-rep-johnson-says-hes-staying-in-governors-race-amid-rumors-of-dropping-bid |access-date=2026-03-21 |website=WCIV |language=en}}</ref> Johnson officially filed for governor on March 23, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 23, 2026 |title=Candidates |url=https://vrems.scvotes.sc.gov/Candidate/CandidateSearch?electionId=22596 |access-date=March 23, 2026 |website=South Carolina Election Commission}}</ref>

On June 9, 2026, Johnson became the Democratic nominee for governor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lund |first=Genevieve      |date=June 10, 2026 |title=South Carolina governor’s race heads to GOP runoff as Democrats select Johnson |url=https://www.wsav.com/news/local-news/south-carolina-news/south-carolina-governors-race-heads-to-gop-runoff-as-democrats-select-johnson/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |publisher=[[WSAV-TV|WSAV]].com}}</ref> On July 30, 2026, Charleston businessman Sam Skardon was announced as Johnson's running mate.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grenier |first=Ian |date=2026-07-30 |title=Charleston Democrat picked as gubernatorial nominee Jermaine Johnson’s Lt. Gov. running mate |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/jermaine-johnson-sam-skardon-sc-demoractic-governor/article_3b7cd8b1-d9c8-41ff-b1e8-e5c928a5c998.html |access-date=2026-07-30 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref>

== Political Views ==

=== Education ===
Johnson supports increased investment in public education and opposes directing public funds toward [[Private school vouchers|private-school vouchers]] until South Carolina's public education system has been improved. He has proposed a statewide audit of [[K–12 education|K-12 education]] to evaluate school performance, funding, and administrative costs. He also supports expanding [[trade schools]] and technical education programs, drawing on his work establishing the Lower Richland Tech Academy.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Healthcare ===
Johnson supports expanding [[Medicaid]] eligibility and improving access to healthcare in rural communities. His gubernatorial platform proposes funding additional rural hospitals and increasing investment in mental-health services. He also has advocated legalizing [[medical cannabis]] as a treatment option.<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Abortion and reproductive rights ===
Johnson supports protecting [[reproductive rights]] and opposes further restrictions on [[abortion]]. He has argued that medical decisions concerning reproductive health should be made by patients, their families, and their doctors. He has also proposed giving female legislator a central role in developing legislation concerning women's reproductive rights.<ref name="Website-1" />

=== Criminal justice and public safety ===
Johnson supports investing in law enforcement while expanding community-based approaches to public safety. His platform proposes increasing law-enforcement staffing and salaries, establishing mental-health response teams to handle certain crises without armed police intervention, and strengthening relationships between law enforcement and local communities. He also supports a state [[Hate crime laws in the United States|hate-crimes law]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Infrastructure and economic development ===
Johnson has made roads, bridges, broadband access, and rural economic development central to his political agenda. He supports allowing [[Municipality|municipalities]] greater flexibility to repair local roads using state transportation funds and has proposed requiring the state to develop greater in-state production capacity for food, energy, and water resources. He has also called for a statewide [[Moratorium (law)|moratorium]] on new data centers until lawmakers establish regulations addressing their resource use, energy demands, and effects on local communities.<ref name="Website-1" />

=== Immigration ===
Johnson has expressed support for [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|immigration enforcement]] conducted in coordination with local authorities. During the 2026 Democratic gubernatorial primary, he and other candidates criticized aspects of federal Immigration and Customs Enforcement operations that they considered insufficiently coordinated with local communities. Johnson's position, as reported during the primary, emphasized cooperation between federal and local authorities rather than uncoordinated enforcement actions.<ref name="Website-1" />

=== Civil rights and equality ===
Johnson opposed an anti-[[diversity, equity, and inclusion]] provision in the South Carolina Legislature and supports enacting a state hate-crimes law. His platform presents the latter as a measure to protect residents from crimes motivated by prejudice.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Data centers and energy ===
Johnson supports a statewide pause on the construction of new [[Data center|data centers]] until South Carolina establishes a regulatory framework governing their energy and water consumption and community impacts. He has argued that companies developing data centers should bear greater responsibility for the resources their facilities require. His position is focused on establishing safeguards before additional projects proceed.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Family Policy ===
Johnson supports policies intended to strengthen family involvement and parental participation in children's lives. His platform proposes encouraging employers to provide paid family leave for fathers, promoting flexible work schedules for parents, and establishing a Governor's Commission on Fathers and Families to coordinate related policies.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Policies {{!}} Explore Policy Solutions — Make A Difference |url=https://johnsonforsc.com/policies |access-date=2026-10-17 |website=Dr. Jermaine Johnson for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

